// ============================================================================
// lib/string.bx — Strings, beyond the four builtins.
//
//     use "lib/string.bx";
//
// A Burxt String is bytes, and the language gives you `len`, `byte_at`,
// `substring` and `+`. Everything here is written from those four, so nothing in
// this file can do something a program could not have done itself — which is the
// point of a standard library rather than a privileged one.
// ============================================================================

use "option.bx";

// ============================================================================
// BUILDING TEXT IN A LOOP — §D0, and it is a decision rather than a preference.
//
// **A chunk list, joined PAIRWISE.** Accumulate pieces into a pending String, push it into a
// `[String]` once it passes `STRING_CHUNK` bytes, and join the list by repeated pairwise merge.
// Every function in this file that builds a String does exactly this, and the three measurements
// below are why it is not up to the author of the next one.
//
// A Burxt String is immutable, so `out += piece` allocates a whole new String holding everything
// collected so far. Doing that per piece is quadratic. Measured on this compiler, building 100 KB
// one byte at a time in a region:
//
//     out += piece, flat                     4 GB arena EXHAUSTED, process dead, 2.71 s
//     a pending chunk flushed into a flat String at 4096      202 MB, 0.06 s
//     a chunk list joined pairwise, flushed at 32              10 MB, 0.00 s
//
// The middle row is the idiom this file used until now — `string_reverse`, the two case functions
// and `from_bytes` all had it — and it is 20× the chunk list, because the flat `done` it flushes
// into is still quadratic in the number of flushes. Halving the work is not the same as changing
// the shape.
//
// **The join must be pairwise.** Folding left rebuilds the whole prefix at every step, which is
// the same quadratic one level up.
//
// ---- the threshold is MEASURED, and the number here is not emit.bx's ----------------------
//
// `src/burxt-compiler/emit.bx` flushes at 128 and `write_module`'s threshold turned out inert.
// Neither number transfers, because **the right threshold depends on the size of the piece being
// appended**, and every function in this file appends ONE BYTE or one codepoint where the emitter
// appends whole IR lines. Building 1 MB one byte at a time:
//
//     no pending chunk, one list entry per byte    66 MB
//     flush at 16                                 40 MB
//     flush at 32                                 46 MB
//     flush at 64                                 60 MB
//     flush at 128                                90 MB
//     flush at 512                               275 MB
//     flush at 4096                            2,021 MB
//
// Two costs pull against each other: the pending chunk is quadratic in the threshold, so a large
// one is dear; and the pairwise join costs one full copy per merge pass, so a small threshold buys
// a longer list and more passes. The optimum is flat across 16..32 and the analysis says why —
// for a piece of `p` bytes and output `n`, the pending cost is `n * T / (2p)` and the join is
// `n * log2(n / T)`. **32 is chosen over the marginally better 16 because it halves the list for
// the callers that append 2-, 3- and 4-byte codepoints, at a measured 6 MB per megabyte.**
//
// ---- a Builder CLASS was tried, and the language refused it -------------------------------
//
// The obvious tidy-up is `class Builder { pieces: [String], pending: String }` with an `add`
// method, putting the discipline in one place instead of six lines per function. **It cannot be
// written**, and the reason is a real rule rather than a gap:
//
//   * `add` must take `mutable self`, and **`pure` and `mutable self` are mutually exclusive** —
//     the compiler says so and gives the reason: a contract clause may call a `pure` method, so
//     allowing both would let a precondition rewrite the value it is checking.
//   * Purity is transitive, so a `pure` function may not call a non-`pure` method. Most functions
//     in this file are `pure` and that is worth keeping: it is what lets them appear in a
//     `requires` clause, as `char_count` does in `char_at`'s.
//   * A constructor function cannot return one either — a class holding an array is region
//     storage, so `function builder() -> Builder` is refused for escaping its region.
//
// So the six lines are written out. Recorded because it is exactly the change someone would make
// again on principle, and the third bullet alone would send them looking for a compiler bug.
//
// ---- one thing D0 says that this file MEASURED as no longer true ---------------------------
//
// `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §D0's third bullet says *"a String's `len` walks it, so never leave
// `len(s)` in a loop condition"*, and a comment in `src/burxt-compiler/emit.bx` says the same. **It
// does not walk it.** `build_str_len` in `src/rust-compiler/codegen.rs` loads the length from the
// eight bytes before the first byte — one load, no scan — and its own comment dates the change:
// *"this was a `strlen` until v0.0.120, which is why reading n bytes cost n²"*.
//
// Measured rather than read: summing every byte of a 1.3 MB String with `while k < len(chunk)` in
// the condition takes 0.00 s, and is indistinguishable from the same loop with the length hoisted.
// A scan per iteration would be 1.7 × 10^12 byte reads and would take minutes.
//
// The advice is harmless and the functions here hoist the length anyway, because it reads better
// and costs nothing. But the REASON given for it is stale by 159 versions, and a stale reason is
// worse than none: it is the kind of note that gets cited to justify contorting the next function.
// The `len` warning is the one part of D0 not to inherit. Flagged here rather than corrected in the
// spec, which this file does not own.
// ============================================================================

// The flush point. See the sweep above; it is 32 because that was measured, not because it is a
// round number.
const STRING_CHUNK: Int = 32;

// A list of pieces into the one String they spell, by repeated PAIRWISE merge.
//
// Public, because it is the other half of the idiom above: a caller writing its own loop needs
// this to finish, and a private one would mean every caller re-deriving the merge — which is how
// the left fold gets written by accident.
//
// `log2(n)` passes, each copying every byte exactly once, so `n log n` bytes moved in total
// against the flat fold's `n²`. The `while len(joined) > 1` shape is `join_chunks` in
// `src/burxt-compiler/emit.bx`, which is the reference implementation and was measured first.
//
// It does not mutate its argument: the first pass READS `chunks` and writes a fresh `merged`, and
// `joined` is rebound rather than pushed into. That matters because an array is a handle — pushing
// into `joined` on the first pass would grow the caller's own list.
pure function string_join_chunks(chunks: [String]) -> String {
    if len(chunks) == 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let mutable joined: [String] = chunks;
    while len(joined) > 1 {
        let mutable merged: [String] = [];
        let mutable i: Int = 0;
        while i + 1 < len(joined) {
            push(merged, joined[i] + joined[i + 1]);
            i += 2;
        }
        // An odd one out rides to the next pass unmerged rather than being folded into the last
        // pair, which would make that pair two pieces wide and the pass uneven.
        if i < len(joined) {
            push(merged, joined[i]);
        }
        joined = merged;
    }
    return joined[0];
}

// Where `needle` first appears in `text`, or -1. The naive scan, deliberately:
// a compiler's lexer does not search, and a program that needs Boyer-Moore knows
// it does. Simple until measured.
pure function string_find(text: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    let n: Int = len(needle);
    if n == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i + n <= len(text) {
        let mutable k: Int = 0;
        let mutable same: Bool = true;
        while k < n {
            if byte_at(text, i + k) != byte_at(needle, k) {
                same = false;
                k = n;
            } else {
                k += 1;
            }
        }
        if same {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

pure function string_contains(text: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    return string_find(text, needle) >= 0;
}

pure function string_starts_with(text: String, prefix: String) -> Bool {
    if len(prefix) > len(text) {
        return false;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(prefix) {
        if byte_at(text, i) != byte_at(prefix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

pure function string_ends_with(text: String, suffix: String) -> Bool {
    let n: Int = len(suffix);
    if n > len(text) {
        return false;
    }
    let base: Int = len(text) - n;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        if byte_at(text, base + i) != byte_at(suffix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Whitespace removed from both ends: space, tab, newline, carriage return.
pure function string_trim(text: String) -> String {
    let mutable from: Int = 0;
    while from < len(text) && string_is_space(byte_at(text, from)) {
        from += 1;
    }
    let mutable to: Int = len(text);
    while to > from && string_is_space(byte_at(text, to - 1)) {
        to -= 1;
    }
    return substring(text, from, to - from);
}

pure function string_is_space(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return byte == 32 || byte == 9 || byte == 10 || byte == 13;
}

// Split on a separator STRING. Answers a growable array, so the pieces are new Strings and they have
// to live somewhere.
//
// It took an `Int` until v0.0.188 — `string_split(text, 44)` for a comma — which meant a caller had to
// know ASCII codes, and `", "` and `"\r\n"` could not be split on at all. That is not an
// inconvenience, it is ordinary text handling out of reach: CSV with spaces after the commas, and any
// file written on Windows.
//
// One spelling per concept, so the byte form is gone rather than kept beside this. A separator is a
// string; that a one-character string is also a byte is not a second idea.
//
// An EMPTY separator answers the whole text as one piece rather than splitting into characters.
//
// ~~Burxt has no character type — a String is bytes — so "split into characters" is not a thing this
// could mean.~~ **That reason expired with A5 (v0.0.250).** A one-codepoint String is exactly what
// `char_at` answers, so "split into characters" now means something precise. The BEHAVIOUR here is
// unchanged and the decision is deliberately still open; what follows is the argument, not a ruling.
//
// The case for leaving it: an empty separator has **no forced answer**. It occurs between every pair
// of characters, and also at the start, at the end, and unboundedly often at each position — so
// "cut at every occurrence" does not determine a result. Python refuses `"abc".split("")` outright;
// JavaScript answers UTF-16 code units and cuts emoji in half. Neither is a spelling worth copying.
//
// And when there is more than one right answer, this project's habit is to make the caller pick by
// NAME — `divide_floor` against `divide_toward_zero`, `shift_right_zeros` against
// `shift_right_sign`, `string_to_upper_ascii` against a full mapping that does not exist yet. By
// that habit the codepoint split is a separate `string_chars(text) -> [String]`, six lines over
// `next_char`, and not a degenerate case of this one. It is not written, because which way this
// goes is not a call to make inside a comment.
//
// The one thing that would be wrong is silence: looping forever on a zero-width match.
function string_split(text: String, separator: String) -> [String] {
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let width: Int = len(separator);
    if width == 0 {
        let whole: Int = push(pieces, text);
        return pieces;
    }
    let mutable from: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i <= len(text) - width {
        if string_matches_at(text, separator, i) {
            let added: Int = push(pieces, substring(text, from, i - from));
            i += width;
            from = i;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    // The tail, always — so "a,b," answers three pieces, the last of them empty. A trailing separator
    // means an empty final field, which is what every CSV reader has to agree on.
    let last: Int = push(pieces, substring(text, from, len(text) - from));
    return pieces;
}

// Does `needle` sit at `at` in `text`? Compared in place, because `substring` would allocate once per
// position and a split has no business needing a region.
pure function string_matches_at(text: String, needle: String, at: Int) -> Bool {
    let width: Int = len(needle);
    if at < 0 || at + width > len(text) {
        return false;
    }
    let mutable k: Int = 0;
    while k < width {
        if byte_at(text, at + k) != byte_at(needle, k) {
            return false;
        }
        k += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Lines, on either ending. A CRLF file and an LF file split identically, which is the whole reason a
// multi-character separator had to exist: `"\r\n"` could not be written as a byte.
function string_lines(text: String) -> [String] {
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let pieces: [String] = string_split(text, "\n");
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(pieces) {
        let piece: String = pieces[i];
        // A bare `\r` before the newline belongs to the line ending, not to the line.
        if len(piece) > 0 && byte_at(piece, len(piece) - 1) == 13 {
            let added: Int = push(out, substring(piece, 0, len(piece) - 1));
        } else {
            let added: Int = push(out, piece);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// ---- Reading a number out of text ------------------------------------------
//
// Two functions, and the difference is whether a fallback is the right answer. That is the
// same pair `lib/map.bx` offers for a missing key — `get(key, fallback)` and `find(key)` — and
// it is deliberately NOT a `Result`: parsing a number is the most ordinary thing a program
// does with input, and making the ordinary case cost a `match` or a `?` would be the language
// charging you for its own strictness.
//
// It used to be one function returning a bare `Int`, and it answered **0** for anything it
// could not read. `"0"` and `"not a number"` were indistinguishable — a silent wrong answer, in
// the standard library of a language whose whole thesis is refusing them. Fixed in v0.0.152.

// The number, or the fallback you chose. Use this when a default is genuinely right — a
// missing count is zero, a missing page number is one.
//
//     let port: Int = string_to_int(configured, 8080);
function string_to_int(text: String, fallback: Int) -> Int {
    match string_parse_int(text) {
        None => { return fallback; }
        Some(n) => { return n; }
    }
}

// The number, or nothing — for when there is no sensible default and the program has to say
// what it does about bad input.
//
//     match string_parse_int(field) {
//         None    => { print("not a number: " + field); }
//         Some(n) => { print(n * 2); }
//     }
//
// **One line on top of `string_parse_int_base`, since v0.0.279, and that is a bug fix rather than a
// tidy-up.** The hand-written base-10 accumulator this used to hold was `value = value * 10 + digit`
// with no overflow guard, and Burxt's `*` traps — so `string_parse_int("99999999999999999999")`
// did not answer None, it **ended the process** with `arithmetic overflow` and exit code 70.
// Measured. See the §D1d section header for why that was worse than the silent 0 this function was
// fixed for in v0.0.152, and for how the guard below it works.
pure function string_parse_int(text: String) -> Option<Int> {
    return string_parse_int_base(text, 10);
}

// The separator between each piece. The join a program would write, written once.
//
// The §D0 chunk list. This was `out += separator + pieces[i]` until the D1 work measured it, which
// made joining a 100 KB document out of its lines cost the square of its length — in the one
// function a program reaches for precisely because it has a lot of pieces.
function string_join(pieces: [String], separator: String) -> String {
    let count: Int = len(pieces);
    if count == 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let mutable chunks: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < count {
        if i > 0 {
            chunk += separator;
        }
        chunk += pieces[i];
        if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
            push(chunks, chunk);
            chunk = "";
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(chunks, chunk);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(chunks);
}

// `text` repeated `times` over. `string_repeat("ab", 3)` is `"ababab"`.
//
// **By DOUBLING, not by a chunk list, and this is the one place in the file where the chunk list is
// not the right answer.** `out += text` in a loop was the original and is quadratic. A chunk list
// would fix that and copy `n log(times)` bytes; doubling copies `2n` and needs no list at all,
// because a repeat is the one builder that already knows every piece is identical. The same binary
// decomposition `math_pow` uses: `doubled` holds `text` repeated a power-of-two number of times,
// and the bits of `times` say which powers to keep.
//
// `times <= 0` answers `""` rather than refusing. Zero copies of something is a real and useful
// answer — it is what `string_pad_start` asks for when the text already fills the width — and a
// negative count is the same question asked clumsily. This is the case where a fallback is right
// and a `requires` would make every caller guard a value it does not care about.
pure function string_repeat(text: String, times: Int) -> String {
    if times <= 0 || len(text) == 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let mutable out: String = "";
    let mutable doubled: String = text;
    let mutable left: Int = times;
    while left > 0 {
        if remainder(left, 2) == 1 {
            out = out + doubled;
        }
        left = divide_toward_zero(left, 2);
        // Guarded, so the last pass does not double a String it will never use — which for a large
        // `text` is the difference between the answer's size and twice it.
        if left > 0 {
            doubled = doubled + doubled;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

// ============================================================================
// CODEPOINTS — roadmap A5, and it needed nothing from the compiler.
//
// `spec/A4.4` calls codepoint iteration its one remaining gap and `ROADMAP-1.0.md` files it under
// §A, compiler leverage. **It is not a compiler item.** Everything below is written from `byte_at`,
// `bit_and`, `substring` and `len` — the four things this file's header already promises — and both
// compilers accepted it unchanged on the first try. Measured before anything was touched, which is
// the only reason it is worth saying: A5 is a LIBRARY item that was mis-filed, and the four
// functions are the whole of it.
//
// `src/burxt-compiler/diag.bx` proved it first and its comment makes the argument: the question is
// not *"can I iterate codepoints"* but *"can I COUNT them"*, and in UTF-8 every byte after the first
// of a sequence matches `10xxxxxx`, so a codepoint count is the number of bytes that are **not**
// continuation bytes. No iterator, no char type, no new scalar.
//
// ---- the two decisions this obeys ------------------------------------------------------
//
// **No char type.** A single codepoint is a ONE-CODEPOINT STRING, not a new kind of value. So
// `char_at` answers a String and there is nothing new to teach `print`, `==`, `+` or a `match`
// about — which is the whole saving. `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md`'s decisions list says *"no char type, no
// bare `s[i]`"*, and the second half is why there is no `s[i]` here either.
//
// **Explicit views rather than bare indexing.** `spec/A4.4` wants `.bytes()` / `.chars()` because
// bare indexing *"hides the silent-wrong-index class of bug"* — is `s[3]` the fourth byte or the
// fourth character? `len`/`byte_at` are the byte view and these are the codepoint view, and a
// reader can see which one they are in from the NAME rather than from the type.
//
// ---- how to walk, and why `next_char` exists ------------------------------------------
//
// There is no iterator protocol yet (A11), and half of one would be worse than none, so a walk is a
// plain `while`:
//
//     let mutable at: Int = 0;
//     while at < len(text) {
//         let ending: Int = next_char(text, at);
//         print(substring(text, at, ending - at));
//         at = ending;
//     }
//
// **`char_at` in a loop is O(n²) and this is not.** `char_at(s, i)` has to walk from the start to
// find the i'th codepoint, so counting up through it re-walks the prefix every time. `next_char`
// carries the position forward instead, which is what makes the other three cheap. Use `char_at`
// when you want ONE codepoint by position; use `next_char` when you want all of them.
//
// ---- what these assume about validity -------------------------------------------------
//
// `spec/A4.4` declares a String is UTF-8 and `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §B5 records that the invariant is
// **declared and unenforced** at four entry points. That is not fixed here, so the honest statement
// is which of these rely on it:
//
//   * `char_count` and `char_at` ASSUME validity. On invalid bytes they answer something
//     well-defined but meaningless — a count of non-continuation bytes is not a codepoint count if
//     the bytes are not UTF-8.
//   * `next_char` assumes NOTHING and is total. An unexpected byte advances by one, which is the
//     standard resynchronisation, and the answer is clamped to `len(s)` so a truncated sequence at
//     the end cannot walk past it. **It never answers `at`**, which is the property a `while` loop
//     depends on: a walk cannot hang, whatever the bytes are.
//   * `is_valid_utf8` assumes nothing — deciding is its job.
// ============================================================================

// How many codepoints, not bytes. `char_count("héllo")` is 5 where `len` is 6.
//
// A continuation byte is `10xxxxxx`, so every byte that is not one begins a codepoint and the count
// is a single pass with no decoding. Assumes valid UTF-8: see the note above.
pure function char_count(text: String) -> Int {
    let mutable found: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) {
        if bit_and(byte_at(text, i), 192) != 128 {
            found += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return found;
}

// The byte offset one past the codepoint that starts at `at` — where the NEXT one begins.
//
// The width is in the leading byte and nowhere else, which is the property that makes UTF-8 walkable
// in one direction without a table: `0xxxxxxx` is one byte, `110xxxxx` two, `1110xxxx` three,
// `11110xxx` four.
//
// Total by construction, and both halves of that matter. It always advances at least one byte, so a
// `while at < len(text)` loop terminates on any input including bytes that are not UTF-8 at all; and
// it never answers past `len(text)`, so `substring(text, at, next_char(text, at) - at)` is in range
// even when the last sequence is truncated. An unexpected byte — a stray continuation, an `0xFF` —
// advances by one, which resynchronises rather than guessing.
pure function next_char(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    if at >= stop {
        return stop;
    }
    let leading: Int = byte_at(text, at);
    let mutable ending: Int = at + 1;
    if bit_and(leading, 224) == 192 {
        ending = at + 2;
    }
    if bit_and(leading, 240) == 224 {
        ending = at + 3;
    }
    if bit_and(leading, 248) == 240 {
        ending = at + 4;
    }
    if ending > stop {
        return stop;
    }
    return ending;
}

// The `i`'th codepoint, as a one-codepoint String. There is no char type: see the header.
//
// A PRECONDITION rather than an empty String for an index out of range, which is this language's
// habit and the better answer here — an empty String would be a legal value that silently stands in
// for a mistake, and a caller comparing it against something would get `false` rather than a
// refusal. `requires` says the mistake out loud, at the call, naming the value.
//
// The second clause calls `char_count`, which is only possible because a contract clause may call a
// `pure` function — and that a `pure` FUNCTION may be called from a clause is older than A4. What A4
// added is the same for a method, so this composes without needing it.
//
// O(n) in `i`, so a loop counting up through it is O(n²). Use `next_char` to walk.
pure function char_at(text: String, i: Int) -> String
    requires i >= 0
    requires i < char_count(text)
{
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    while seen < i {
        at = next_char(text, at);
        seen += 1;
    }
    return substring(text, at, next_char(text, at) - at);
}

// Is every byte part of a well-formed UTF-8 sequence?
//
// The full rule, not the structural half. A checker that accepted overlong encodings and surrogates
// would be a blanket that reads like a rule — the shape that let `?` go unimplemented for its whole
// life — so the second byte's range depends on the leader, which is where the three exclusions live:
//
//   * **overlong**: `C0`/`C1` could only encode something a shorter sequence already can, and `E0`
//     and `F0` restrict the second byte for the same reason. Two spellings of one codepoint is how
//     a validator and a decoder come to disagree about a string.
//   * **surrogates**: `ED A0`..`ED BF` is U+D800..U+DFFF, which UTF-8 does not encode.
//   * **out of range**: past `F4 8F` is above U+10FFFF, which is not a codepoint.
//
// Assumes nothing. This is the function `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §B5 would need if the declared-and-
// unenforced UTF-8 invariant were ever enforced at the four entry points — enforcing it is not done
// here, and having something to enforce it WITH is the part A5 delivers.
pure function is_valid_utf8(text: String) -> Bool {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        let leading: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        let mutable width: Int = 0;
        let mutable low: Int = 128;
        let mutable high: Int = 191;
        if leading < 128 {
            width = 1;
        }
        if leading >= 194 && leading <= 223 {
            width = 2;
        }
        if leading == 224 {
            width = 3;
            low = 160;                               // no overlong three-byte form
        }
        if leading >= 225 && leading <= 236 {
            width = 3;
        }
        if leading == 237 {
            width = 3;
            high = 159;                              // no surrogate
        }
        if leading >= 238 && leading <= 239 {
            width = 3;
        }
        if leading == 240 {
            width = 4;
            low = 144;                               // no overlong four-byte form
        }
        if leading >= 241 && leading <= 243 {
            width = 4;
        }
        if leading == 244 {
            width = 4;
            high = 143;                              // nothing above U+10FFFF
        }
        if width == 0 {
            return false;                            // a continuation byte, or 192, 193, 245..255
        }
        if i + width > stop {
            return false;                            // truncated
        }
        let mutable c: Int = 1;
        while c < width {
            let byte: Int = byte_at(text, i + c);
            // The first continuation byte carries the leader's extra restriction; the rest are the
            // plain `10xxxxxx`.
            let mutable least: Int = 128;
            let mutable most: Int = 191;
            if c == 1 {
                least = low;
                most = high;
            }
            if byte < least || byte > most {
                return false;
            }
            c += 1;
        }
        i += width;
    }
    return true;
}

// A continuation byte — `10xxxxxx`, the second and later byte of a multi-byte sequence, and never
// the first. §D1p asks for it by name, and it is one line, but it is the line every other function
// in this section is built on: `char_count` counts bytes that are NOT this, and `next_char` reads a
// width only from bytes that are NOT this.
//
// Takes a BYTE, not a String, and so is spelled like `string_is_space` — the two are the same kind
// of question asked of the same kind of value.
pure function is_continuation(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return bit_and(byte, 192) == 128;
}

// The codepoint at codepoint index `i`, as a number. `codepoint_at("é", 0)` is 233.
//
// The companion to `char_at`, which answers the same character as a one-codepoint String. Two
// functions rather than one because the two answers are used for different things: a String goes
// into output and a comparison, a number goes into a range test and arithmetic. Neither is
// derivable from the other without the decode below, so having only one would make every caller
// write it.
//
// The width comes from `next_char` rather than from the leading byte a second time, which is what
// makes this total: `next_char` clamps to `len(text)`, so no read below can go past the end even on
// a truncated final sequence. It ASSUMES valid UTF-8 for its ANSWER — a truncated three-byte
// sequence decodes as though it were the two bytes that are there, which is a well-defined number
// and not the codepoint anyone meant. Same standing as `char_count` and `char_at`; see the section
// header.
//
// O(n) in `i` for the same reason `char_at` is. Walk with `next_char` if you want all of them.
pure function codepoint_at(text: String, i: Int) -> Int
    requires i >= 0
    requires i < char_count(text)
{
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    while seen < i {
        at = next_char(text, at);
        seen += 1;
    }
    let ending: Int = next_char(text, at);
    let width: Int = ending - at;
    let leading: Int = byte_at(text, at);
    if width == 1 {
        return leading;
    }
    // The leading byte carries the top bits and each continuation carries six more. The masks are
    // the complement of the width marker: `110xxxxx` keeps 5 bits, `1110xxxx` keeps 4, `11110xxx`
    // keeps 3.
    if width == 2 {
        return shift_left(bit_and(leading, 31), 6)
             + bit_and(byte_at(text, at + 1), 63);
    }
    if width == 3 {
        return shift_left(bit_and(leading, 15), 12)
             + shift_left(bit_and(byte_at(text, at + 1), 63), 6)
             + bit_and(byte_at(text, at + 2), 63);
    }
    return shift_left(bit_and(leading, 7), 18)
         + shift_left(bit_and(byte_at(text, at + 1), 63), 12)
         + shift_left(bit_and(byte_at(text, at + 2), 63), 6)
         + bit_and(byte_at(text, at + 3), 63);
}

// Every byte, as numbers. §D1p asks for it, and `from_bytes` at the bottom of this file is the
// inverse — `from_bytes(to_bytes(s)) == s` for every String. This note used to say the partner did
// not exist and that getting bytes back into a String was "the thing this language cannot do yet";
// the `byte_as_string` builtin (§A13) is what changed, in v0.0.259.
//
// `len` and `byte_at` already give a caller this one byte at a time, so this exists for the case
// where the bytes have to be held: passed to a function, sorted, hashed, compared as a whole.
function to_bytes(text: String) -> [Int] {
    let mutable out: [Int] = [];
    for i in 0..len(text) {
        let added: Int = push(out, byte_at(text, i));
    }
    return out;
}

// The CODEPOINT index of `of`, or None. The companion to `string_find`, which answers a BYTE offset.
//
// **Both exist because they answer different questions, and mixing them corrupts text.** A byte
// offset is what `substring` takes; a codepoint index is what `char_at` takes and what a human
// counting characters means. In `"héllo"` the `l` is at byte 3 and character 2, and handing 3 to
// `char_at` reads the wrong character while handing 2 to `substring` splits the `é` in half. The
// names are the only thing standing between a caller and that bug, which is why neither is called
// `index_of`.
//
// Matches only on a CODEPOINT BOUNDARY, and that is a real difference from `string_find` rather
// than an implementation detail: searching `"é"` for the single byte `0xA9` succeeds byte-wise and
// has no codepoint index to answer, so this walks boundaries and compares from each one. A needle
// that only occurs straddling a character is reported as absent, which is the truthful answer to
// the question actually asked.
//
// An EMPTY needle answers `Some(0)`, matching `string_find`'s 0 — every string contains the empty
// string, at the beginning.
function char_index(text: String, of: String) -> Option<Int> {
    if len(of) == 0 {
        return Option.Some(0);
    }
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable index: Int = 0;
    while at < len(text) {
        if string_matches_at(text, of, at) {
            return Option.Some(index);
        }
        at = next_char(text, at);
        index += 1;
    }
    return Option.None;
}

// ---- reversing, which is what a byte-wise version gets wrong -----------------------------

// The text, codepoint by codepoint, backwards. **`string_reverse("aé中🙂")` is `"🙂中éa"`.**
//
// This is the function §D1a has asked for since the first audit, and the reason it needed A5 is
// that **a byte-wise reverse corrupts every multi-byte character.** Reversing the bytes of `"é"`
// (`C3 A9`) gives `A9 C3`, which is not a character at all — a continuation byte followed by a
// leader. The output is not merely wrong, it is not UTF-8, and every downstream reader of it is
// entitled to do something different with it. Whole codepoints move; their bytes do not.
//
// ---- the limit, which the roadmap's word "codepoint-aware" states exactly ----
//
// This reverses CODEPOINTS, not what a reader would call characters, and the difference is visible:
//
//   * `"e" + U+0301` (a combining acute) renders as `é` and is TWO codepoints. Reversed, the accent
//     lands on whatever now precedes it. Still valid UTF-8, still the wrong picture.
//   * An emoji built from a zero-width-joiner sequence, or a flag built from two regional
//     indicators, comes apart the same way.
//
// The fix is grapheme-cluster segmentation, which needs the Unicode break tables — a generated
// data file, not a library function, and the same class of thing full case mapping needs. It is not
// here and this is not pretending otherwise. What IS guaranteed: the output is valid UTF-8 whenever
// the input is, and reversing twice returns the original exactly. Both are checked in
// `tests/pass/string_codepoints.bx` on text with two-, three- and four-byte characters.
//
// ---- the cost ----
//
// The §D0 chunk list, and this function is where the difference between it and the 4 KB two-level
// version it replaced is easiest to see. A String is immutable, so `out = piece + out` copies
// everything collected so far on every character and turns a reverse into O(n²); a pending chunk
// bounds that copy, but flushing it into a flat `done` is quadratic again in the number of flushes.
// Both levels have to be a list. See the section at the top of this file for the numbers.
//
// **The chunks come out in reverse order and are reversed again to join**, which is the one thing
// here that is not the plain idiom: each chunk holds its own bytes already reversed, so the whole
// answer is the LAST chunk first.
pure function string_reverse(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    while at < stop {
        let ending: Int = next_char(text, at);
        // PREPEND: the piece read last ends up first. Bounded by STRING_CHUNK, so this copy is a
        // constant rather than the length of everything collected.
        chunk = substring(text, at, ending - at) + chunk;
        if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
            push(pieces, chunk);
            chunk = "";
        }
        at = ending;
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(pieces, chunk);
    }
    let mutable backwards: [String] = [];
    let mutable k: Int = len(pieces);
    while k > 0 {
        k -= 1;
        push(backwards, pieces[k]);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(backwards);
}

// ---- case, and the name is the decision -------------------------------------------------
//
// **`_ascii` is not a shorthand, it is the promise.** These map `a`..`z` and `A`..`Z`, and pass
// every other byte through unchanged. Two measured examples, both in the fixture:
//
//     string_to_upper_ascii("Straße")  ->  "STRAßE"      the ß is not touched
//     string_to_lower_ascii("ÉCOLE")   ->  "École"       the É is not touched
//
// The second one is the whole argument in one line: it looks like it worked. A caller who tests
// with `"ECOLE"` sees `"ecole"` and ships, and the failure surfaces on somebody else's data.
//
// The name carries the limit for the same reason `divide_floor` and `shift_right_zeros` do: there
// is more than one right answer and the caller must pick. A function called `string_to_upper` that
// silently ignored every character outside ASCII would be a silent wrong answer in the standard
// library of a language whose whole thesis is refusing them — and it is the wrong answer that is
// hardest to notice, because it is correct on all the test data an English-speaking author thinks
// to write.
//
// What full Unicode case mapping would need, so the gap is sized rather than gestured at:
//
//   * ~1,400 generated mapping entries, which is a data file the build produces, not a function.
//   * **`ß` upper-cases to `SS`** — one codepoint becoming two, so the answer is not the same
//     length in characters and no in-place mapping can express it.
//   * **Locale**: Turkish `i` upper-cases to `İ` (dotted), not `I`, so there is no single correct
//     answer without knowing the language. A function with no locale argument cannot be right.
//
// That is a module with a generated table and a locale parameter, and it is a different decision
// from this one. `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §D1a is satisfied by naming the limit today.
//
// ---- why a BYTE-wise loop is safe here, which is not obvious ----
//
// Every byte of a multi-byte sequence is >= 0x80 and every ASCII letter is < 0x80, so a byte-wise
// pass **cannot touch a non-ASCII character** — there is no byte inside `é` or `中` that could be
// mistaken for a letter. That is a designed property of UTF-8 and the reason these need no
// codepoint walk, unlike `string_reverse`. The fixture checks it on real non-ASCII text rather than
// asserting it here.

// ---- how both of these copy, which is not one byte at a time -----------------------------
//
// The §D0 chunk list, plus one thing worth stating because it changes the cost by two orders of
// magnitude on real text: **the bytes that do NOT change are copied as whole RUNS.** `run` marks
// where the current untouched stretch began, and only a letter that actually maps flushes it with
// a single `substring`.
//
// The consequence is that the cost tracks how much text CHANGES rather than how long it is.
// `string_to_upper_ascii` on text that is already upper case — or on any text with no ASCII
// lower-case letters at all, which includes every all-CJK string — does exactly one `substring`
// and one push, whatever its length. A per-byte loop would have copied it a byte at a time and
// paid the pending-chunk quadratic for nothing.

// `a`..`z` become `A`..`Z`. Every other byte, ASCII or not, is passed through unchanged.
pure function string_to_upper_ascii(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable run: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        let byte: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if byte >= 97 && byte <= 122 {
            if i > run {
                chunk += substring(text, run, i - run);
            }
            chunk += ascii_letter(byte - 32);
            run = i + 1;
            if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
                push(pieces, chunk);
                chunk = "";
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if stop > run {
        chunk += substring(text, run, stop - run);
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(pieces, chunk);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(pieces);
}

// `A`..`Z` become `a`..`z`. Every other byte, ASCII or not, is passed through unchanged.
pure function string_to_lower_ascii(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable run: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        let byte: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if byte >= 65 && byte <= 90 {
            if i > run {
                chunk += substring(text, run, i - run);
            }
            chunk += ascii_letter(byte + 32);
            run = i + 1;
            if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
                push(pieces, chunk);
                chunk = "";
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if stop > run {
        chunk += substring(text, run, stop - run);
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(pieces, chunk);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(pieces);
}

// The one-byte String for an ASCII LETTER code.
//
// **Two 26-character tables and a `substring` until v0.0.259**, because there was no way to build a
// String from a byte value in this language and `substring` of a literal was the only Int-to-String
// path there was. It worked here precisely because the 52 letters were the only bytes the two case
// functions ever need to produce. `byte_as_string` (§A13) makes the tables unnecessary, and the body
// is now one line.
//
// **It is kept rather than replaced by the builtin, and the difference is the CONTRACT, not the
// conversion.** The three `requires` clauses say this is a letter code and nothing else, which is
// what makes the two callers above readable: `ascii_letter(byte - 32)` states that the arithmetic
// landed in the letter range, and the compiler checks it. `byte_as_string(byte - 32)` would accept
// any byte and say nothing. So this is a narrowing, unlike `from_byte`, which would have been a
// second spelling and is not written.
//
// `requires` rather than a fallback: a code outside the two letter runs is a caller mistake, and
// answering `"?"` for it is how `os_byte_as_string` came to destroy data silently (roadmap §B2).
pure function ascii_letter(code: Int) -> String
    requires code >= 65
    requires code <= 122
    requires code <= 90 || code >= 97
{
    return byte_as_string(code);
}

// ---- cheap classification, §D1c ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// All three answer a question about the WHOLE String and all three are true of the empty String,
// which is the vacuous answer and the one that composes: "every byte is a digit" is true when there
// are no bytes. A caller who means "a non-empty run of digits" checks the length too, and that is
// clearer written at the call than hidden in one of these.

// Is every byte below 128? Equivalently: is this text unchanged by any of the byte-wise functions
// above, and safe to treat as one byte per character?
pure function is_ascii(text: String) -> Bool {
    for i in 0..len(text) {
        if byte_at(text, i) >= 128 {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

// Is every byte `0`..`9`? **Not a number check** — no sign, no digit grouping, no bounds. It says
// what its name says, and `string_parse_int` is the one that answers whether the text is an Int.
// Arabic-Indic and Devanagari digits are NOT digits here, which follows from every byte being ASCII
// and is the same limit `_ascii` names elsewhere.
pure function all_digits(text: String) -> Bool {
    for i in 0..len(text) {
        let byte: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if byte < 48 || byte > 57 {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

// Is every byte an ASCII letter? Same ASCII-only limit: `é` is not alphabetic to this function, and
// a full answer needs the Unicode category tables that full case mapping needs.
pure function is_alpha(text: String) -> Bool {
    for i in 0..len(text) {
        let byte: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if !(byte >= 65 && byte <= 90) && !(byte >= 97 && byte <= 122) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

// ============================================================================
// BUILDING TEXT FROM NUMBERS, §D1p — and this section was THE GAP until v0.0.255.
//
// What stood here was an argument that `from_codepoint`, `from_bytes` and `from_byte` could not be
// written: **there was no way to turn a number into a byte of a String in this language.** Four
// measured steps, kept because they are the reason the builtin below exists rather than a
// convenience — no builtin was Int-to-String; no escape named a byte (`\xNN` and `\uXXXX` do not
// exist); so `substring` of a LITERAL was the only path; and a source file must be valid UTF-8, so
// a byte >= 0x80 could only reach a literal inside a complete multi-byte character.
//
// That last step is what closed the door. Continuation bytes were cheap — `"ÀÁÂ...ÿ"` yields every
// byte `0x80`..`0xBF` — but the 51 UTF-8 LEAD bytes `0xC2`..`0xF4` each needed their own character
// from their own block, six of them unassigned codepoints (`0xCC` combining marks, `0xEE` Private
// Use Area, `0xF1`..`0xF4` four empty planes). Counted, not estimated. **That table was refused**:
// a reviewer cannot read mixed scripts and invisible codepoints, `git diff` shows mojibake, and an
// editor that normalises Unicode would silently break a whole plane.
//
// **The answer was to give the language the missing operation instead: `byte_as_string(n)`**, a
// builtin, roadmap §A13. Everything below is ordinary Burxt on top of it. The refusal was right and
// the workaround would have been wrong; what was missing was one primitive, and a library note is
// where that became visible.
//
// ---- what these can and cannot promise --------------------------------------------------
//
// `byte_as_string` is the ONE builtin that can build a String `is_valid_utf8` rejects —
// `byte_as_string(0xC3)` alone is a lead byte with no continuation, and that is measured, not
// inferred. **`from_codepoint` is the safe layer**: it emits a whole sequence or refuses, so it
// cannot leave a partial one behind. `from_bytes` makes no such promise and says so — it is for
// bytes that are already known to be text, or for building a String that is deliberately binary.

// A codepoint as its UTF-8 bytes. `from_codepoint(233)` is `"é"`, and it is the exact inverse of
// `codepoint_at` — which is the property the fixture checks, over every codepoint in every one of
// the four widths rather than a chosen few.
//
// **Four branches, mirroring `codepoint_at`'s decode.** The leading byte carries the width marker
// and the top bits; each continuation carries six more, marked `10xxxxxx`. Written as the inverse of
// the decoder directly above it so the pair can be read together — a mask there is a shift here.
//
// `requires` rather than a fallback, and this is the same call `ascii_letter` records: a number
// that is not a codepoint is a caller mistake, and answering U+FFFD for it would be the silent
// substitution `os_byte_as_string`'s `"?"` was. The two exclusions are what UTF-8 cannot encode:
//
//   * **above U+10FFFF** there is no codepoint, so there is no encoding to produce.
//   * **U+D800..U+DFFF are SURROGATES** — half of a UTF-16 pair, never a character on their own.
//     Encoding one produces CESU-8, which `is_valid_utf8` rejects, so allowing it here would let
//     this function build text its own module calls invalid.
//
// **It never emits a PARTIAL sequence**, which is the whole reason to prefer it over the builtin:
// every branch appends all of its bytes or the contract refuses before any of them. A caller
// assembling text one codepoint at a time therefore cannot produce a truncated character.
pure function from_codepoint(code: Int) -> String allocates
    requires code >= 0
    requires code <= 1114111
    requires code < 55296 || code > 57343
{
    if code < 128 {
        return byte_as_string(code);
    }
    if code < 2048 {
        return byte_as_string(192 + shift_right_zeros(code, 6))
             + byte_as_string(128 + bit_and(code, 63));
    }
    if code < 65536 {
        return byte_as_string(224 + shift_right_zeros(code, 12))
             + byte_as_string(128 + bit_and(shift_right_zeros(code, 6), 63))
             + byte_as_string(128 + bit_and(code, 63));
    }
    return byte_as_string(240 + shift_right_zeros(code, 18))
         + byte_as_string(128 + bit_and(shift_right_zeros(code, 12), 63))
         + byte_as_string(128 + bit_and(shift_right_zeros(code, 6), 63))
         + byte_as_string(128 + bit_and(code, 63));
}

// Bytes back into a String, the exact inverse of `to_bytes`. `from_bytes(to_bytes(s)) == s` for
// every String, including one holding a NUL — checked in the fixture, because a length-prefixed
// String makes that ordinary and it would be easy to assume otherwise.
//
// **It does NOT promise valid UTF-8**, and that is not a gap to fill later: the bytes are the
// caller's, and a function that validated them would have to answer something for the invalid case,
// which is either a lie or an `Option` that every caller with known-good bytes then has to unwrap.
// `is_valid_utf8(from_bytes(xs))` says it in one line where a reader can see it. This is also the
// only way to build a deliberately BINARY String — which `write_bytes` then writes out.
//
// The §D0 chunk list, not `out += b`, for the reason the section at the top of this file measures:
// appending to a String copies it, so byte-at-a-time is O(n²) and a flat flush target is O(n²) in
// the flushes. There is no run to copy wholesale here — every byte is a separate `[Int]` element
// and has to be converted — so this is the plain idiom.
pure function from_bytes(xs: [Int]) -> String {
    let count: Int = len(xs);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < count {
        chunk += byte_as_string(xs[i]);
        if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
            push(pieces, chunk);
            chunk = "";
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(pieces, chunk);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(pieces);
}

// `from_byte` is NOT here, and §D1p's third name is answered rather than dropped: it would be
// `byte_as_string` under a second spelling. The builtin already takes an Int 0..255 and answers a
// one-byte String, in either compiler, with the same range refusal — there is nothing for a wrapper
// to add, and an alias whose body is `return byte_as_string(n);` costs a reader the question of what
// the difference is. Named here because "we decided it is the same function" and "we forgot" look
// identical from the outside.
// ============================================================================

// ============================================================================
// §D1a — CASE, and the naming rule the section above already stated
//
// `string_to_upper_ascii` and `string_to_lower_ascii` are above, with the argument for why `_ascii`
// is in the name rather than in a footnote. Everything here inherits that limit and the same
// reasoning: **non-ASCII passes through unchanged, and the name is where a caller learns it.**
//
// The two comparison functions allocate NOTHING. That is the point of having them: the obvious way
// to compare case-insensitively is `string_to_lower_ascii(a) == string_to_lower_ascii(b)`, which
// builds two whole Strings in the caller's region to answer a Bool — and in a loop over a word list
// that is the region cost of the entire input, twice, for an answer that needs no memory at all.
// ============================================================================

// One byte, case-folded for comparison: `A`..`Z` become `a`..`z` and every other byte, ASCII or
// not, is itself.
//
// Takes a BYTE, like `string_is_space`, and is the shared kernel of the three `_ignore_case`
// functions. Folding to LOWER rather than upper is arbitrary for ASCII — the two agree on every
// comparison — and is chosen to match what `equals_ignore_case` would do in every other language.
pure function string_fold_ascii(byte: Int) -> Int {
    if byte >= 65 && byte <= 90 {
        return byte + 32;
    }
    return byte;
}

// Same text, ASCII case ignored. Allocates nothing; see the section note.
//
// **A length check first, and it is a real shortcut rather than an optimisation.** ASCII folding
// never changes a byte's length, so two Strings that differ only in ASCII case have the same byte
// length — which means an early `false` on differing lengths cannot be wrong.
pure function string_equals_ignore_case(a: String, b: String) -> Bool {
    let width: Int = len(a);
    if width != len(b) {
        return false;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < width {
        if string_fold_ascii(byte_at(a, i)) != string_fold_ascii(byte_at(b, i)) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// -1, 0 or 1: is `a` before, equal to, or after `b`?
//
// **Byte-wise, and for UTF-8 that IS codepoint order** — a designed property of the encoding rather
// than a coincidence, and the reason this needs no codepoint walk. A shorter String that is a
// prefix of a longer one sorts first, which is the ordinary lexicographic rule.
//
// Three-way rather than the `<` the language already has, because a sort or a binary search wants
// one comparison and not two. It is *not* a second spelling of `<`: `a < b` answers one of the
// three questions and this answers which of the three it is.
pure function string_compare(a: String, b: String) -> Int {
    let width_a: Int = len(a);
    let width_b: Int = len(b);
    let mutable shared: Int = width_a;
    if width_b < shared {
        shared = width_b;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < shared {
        let x: Int = byte_at(a, i);
        let y: Int = byte_at(b, i);
        if x < y {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        if x > y {
            return 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if width_a < width_b {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    if width_a > width_b {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// `string_compare`, with ASCII case folded away. Allocates nothing.
//
// Note what this is NOT: a case-insensitive ORDER for non-ASCII text. `"Ä"` and `"ä"` compare
// unequal here and always will without the Unicode tables. The `_ascii` limit again.
pure function string_compare_ignore_case(a: String, b: String) -> Int {
    let width_a: Int = len(a);
    let width_b: Int = len(b);
    let mutable shared: Int = width_a;
    if width_b < shared {
        shared = width_b;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < shared {
        let x: Int = string_fold_ascii(byte_at(a, i));
        let y: Int = string_fold_ascii(byte_at(b, i));
        if x < y {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        if x > y {
            return 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if width_a < width_b {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    if width_a > width_b {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// `string_find`, with ASCII case folded away. A BYTE offset, like `string_find`, and -1 for absent.
// Allocates nothing — the point of it existing beside `string_find` rather than callers lowering
// both sides first.
pure function string_find_ignore_case(text: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    let width: Int = len(needle);
    if width == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i + width <= stop {
        let mutable k: Int = 0;
        let mutable same: Bool = true;
        while k < width && same {
            if string_fold_ascii(byte_at(text, i + k)) != string_fold_ascii(byte_at(needle, k)) {
                same = false;
            }
            k += 1;
        }
        if same {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// First character upper-cased, **and the rest lower-cased**. `string_capitalise("jOHN")` is
// `"John"`, not `"JOHN"`.
//
// ---- the decision, because there are two defensible answers ----
//
// Lower-casing the tail is Python's `capitalize` and it is chosen here for one reason: it is the
// only answer that makes this and `string_title_case` agree, and a pair of functions that disagree
// about what "capitalise" means is worse than either choice. `"jOHN sMITH"` has to become
// `"John Smith"` under `title_case` — nobody wants `"JOHN SMITH"` — so `capitalise` on one word
// must do the same thing to be its single-word case.
//
// A caller who wants *only* the first character touched has two lines and no ambiguity:
//
//     let head: String = string_to_upper_ascii(char_at(name, 0));
//     let rest: String = substring(name, len(head), len(name) - len(head));
//
// ---- the empty and multi-byte cases ----
//
// The empty String answers itself. A first character that is multi-byte is unchanged, because
// `_ascii` case mapping touches no byte >= 128 — and this splits the text at BYTE 1, so a
// two-byte `é` is briefly two invalid halves that concatenate back to exactly what they were.
// Nothing inspects the halves, and the answer is correct; the fixture checks it on `"élan"`.
pure function string_capitalise(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    if stop == 0 {
        return text;
    }
    let first: Int = byte_at(text, 0);
    let mutable head: String = substring(text, 0, 1);
    if first >= 97 && first <= 122 {
        head = ascii_letter(first - 32);
    }
    return head + string_to_lower_ascii(substring(text, 1, stop - 1));
}

// The first character of every word upper-cased and the rest lower-cased, ASCII only.
//
// ---- what counts as a word boundary, which is the whole content of this function ----
//
// A word STARTS at a byte that is word-ish and follows one that is not. Word-ish means ASCII
// alphanumeric **or any byte >= 128**, and that second clause is the case a naive version gets
// wrong rather than a detail:
//
//   * Every byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 character is >= 128. Treating those as non-word-ish would
//     make the byte AFTER a `é` a word start — so `"élan"` would come out `"éLan"`. Measured on
//     exactly that input before the clause was added.
//   * So `é` is word-ish here even though this function cannot case-map it. That is the honest
//     ASCII-limited answer: it does not claim to know the character's case, only that it is part
//     of a word and does not end one.
//
// Punctuation and digits therefore behave like this, all of which the fixture pins:
//
//     "o'brien"      ->  "O'Brien"      an apostrophe ends a word, as it does in Python
//     "3rd place"    ->  "3rd Place"    a digit is word-ish, so `r` is not a word start
//     "hello-world"  ->  "Hello-World"
//
// The apostrophe case is the one people disagree about. It is recorded rather than argued: a rule
// that made `'` word-ish would give `"O'brien"`, and neither is wrong for every name.
pure function string_title_case(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable in_word: Bool = false;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        let byte: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        let wordish: Bool = string_is_alnum(byte) || byte >= 128;
        let mutable mapped: Int = byte;
        if wordish && !in_word {
            if byte >= 97 && byte <= 122 {
                mapped = byte - 32;
            }
        } else {
            if byte >= 65 && byte <= 90 {
                mapped = byte + 32;
            }
        }
        chunk += byte_as_string(mapped);
        in_word = wordish;
        if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
            push(pieces, chunk);
            chunk = "";
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(pieces, chunk);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(pieces);
}

// ============================================================================
// §D1a — REPLACE
//
// Left to right, non-overlapping, and **the replacement is never rescanned.** That last one is the
// case a wrong implementation passes every ordinary test on and then hangs or explodes:
//
//     string_replace("aaa", "a", "aa")   ->  "aaaaaa"    six, not an infinite loop
//     string_replace("x", "x", "xx")     ->  "xx"        not "xxxx", not forever
//
// The scan advances past the MATCHED text in the source and never looks at what was written, so a
// replacement containing the pattern is inert by construction rather than by a guard. Both are in
// the fixture, because "we thought about it" and "we tested it" are different claims.
//
// An EMPTY pattern answers the text unchanged, which is the same call `string_split` makes for an
// empty separator and for the same reason: an empty match occurs between every pair of characters
// and unboundedly often at each position, so "replace every occurrence" does not determine a
// result. `string_count` answers 0 for it too, so the three agree — a program can ask "how many,
// then replace them all" and not be surprised.
// ============================================================================

// Every occurrence of `from` becomes `to`. The bytes between matches are copied as whole RUNS, so
// a replace that matches rarely costs about one pass whatever the text's length.
pure function string_replace(text: String, from: String, to: String) -> String {
    let width: Int = len(from);
    if width == 0 {
        return text;
    }
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable chunk: String = "";
    let mutable run: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i + width <= stop {
        if string_matches_at(text, from, i) {
            if i > run {
                chunk += substring(text, run, i - run);
            }
            chunk += to;
            // PAST the match in the SOURCE. This is the line that makes a replacement containing
            // the pattern inert.
            i += width;
            run = i;
            if len(chunk) >= STRING_CHUNK {
                push(pieces, chunk);
                chunk = "";
            }
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    if stop > run {
        chunk += substring(text, run, stop - run);
    }
    if len(chunk) > 0 {
        push(pieces, chunk);
    }
    return string_join_chunks(pieces);
}

// The FIRST occurrence of `from` becomes `to`; the rest of the text is untouched.
//
// Not `string_replace` with a counter, because it needs no builder at all: one `find` and two
// substrings, which is three allocations regardless of how long the text is.
pure function string_replace_first(text: String, from: String, to: String) -> String {
    if len(from) == 0 {
        return text;
    }
    let at: Int = string_find(text, from);
    if at < 0 {
        return text;
    }
    let after: Int = at + len(from);
    return substring(text, 0, at) + to + substring(text, after, len(text) - after);
}

// ============================================================================
// §D1a — PAD AND TRIM
//
// **`pad` counts CODEPOINTS and `trim` counts bytes**, and that split is deliberate rather than
// sloppy. Padding exists to line a column up, so it has to count what a reader counts: padding
// `"café"` to 6 with byte arithmetic would add 1 space instead of 2, because the `é` is two bytes,
// and the column would be visibly wrong. Trimming removes ASCII whitespace, and every whitespace
// byte is one byte, so there is nothing for a codepoint walk to add.
//
// This is the `string_find` / `char_index` distinction from earlier in the file, applied to a
// different pair of functions: the units are in the DOCUMENTATION and the behaviour, because the
// types cannot tell them apart.
// ============================================================================

// `fill` repeated on the LEFT until the text is `width` codepoints wide. Already wide enough, or
// wider, answers the text unchanged — never truncates.
//
// **`fill` must be exactly one codepoint**, as a `requires` rather than a silent truncation: a
// two-character fill cannot in general reach a given width at all (`"ab"` can pad to an even number
// of columns and no odd one), so there is no correct answer to give and refusing at the call is the
// only honest option.
pure function string_pad_start(text: String, width: Int, fill: String) -> String
    requires char_count(fill) == 1
{
    let have: Int = char_count(text);
    if have >= width {
        return text;
    }
    return string_repeat(fill, width - have) + text;
}

// `fill` repeated on the RIGHT until the text is `width` codepoints wide. Same contract as
// `string_pad_start`.
pure function string_pad_end(text: String, width: Int, fill: String) -> String
    requires char_count(fill) == 1
{
    let have: Int = char_count(text);
    if have >= width {
        return text;
    }
    return text + string_repeat(fill, width - have);
}

// Whitespace removed from the START only — space, tab, newline, carriage return, the same four
// `string_trim` uses. One `substring`, so it allocates once whatever it removes.
pure function string_trim_start(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable from: Int = 0;
    while from < stop && string_is_space(byte_at(text, from)) {
        from += 1;
    }
    return substring(text, from, stop - from);
}

// Whitespace removed from the END only.
pure function string_trim_end(text: String) -> String {
    let mutable to: Int = len(text);
    while to > 0 && string_is_space(byte_at(text, to - 1)) {
        to -= 1;
    }
    return substring(text, 0, to);
}

// The text without `prefix`, or **the text unchanged** if it does not start with it.
//
// Rust answers an `Option` here and this does not, and the reason is that the question "did it have
// the prefix?" already has a function: `string_starts_with`. An `Option` would make every caller
// who knows the answer write a `match` to get back to the String, and the two-function form lets a
// caller who cares ask and a caller who does not just strip. `string_split_once` answers an
// `Option` because there the "absent" case has no sensible String to return.
pure function string_strip_prefix(text: String, prefix: String) -> String {
    if !string_starts_with(text, prefix) {
        return text;
    }
    let width: Int = len(prefix);
    return substring(text, width, len(text) - width);
}

// The text without `suffix`, or the text unchanged. Same call as `string_strip_prefix`.
pure function string_strip_suffix(text: String, suffix: String) -> String {
    if !string_ends_with(text, suffix) {
        return text;
    }
    return substring(text, 0, len(text) - len(suffix));
}

// ============================================================================
// §D1b — SEARCH AND SPLIT
//
// Everything here answers BYTE offsets, like `string_find` and unlike `char_index`, and the note at
// `char_index` is the argument for why the two families are named apart.
//
// ---- what the three search functions answer for an EMPTY needle ----
//
// It is the same question in three places and the answers have to agree, so they are stated
// together rather than one at a time:
//
//     string_find(text, "")            0        it is there, at the beginning
//     string_find_from(text, "", 7)    7        it is there, where you started looking
//     string_rfind(text, "")           len      it is there, at the end
//     string_count(text, "")           0        "how many" has no forced answer — see below
//
// The first three are forced: the empty String occurs at every position, so the first, the first at
// or after `from`, and the last are exactly 0, `from` and `len`. `string_count` is the one that is
// not forced, and 0 is chosen so that it agrees with `string_replace` and `string_split`, both of
// which treat an empty pattern as matching nothing. Python and Rust answer n+1 here; this file
// prefers three functions that agree with each other over three that each agree with Python.
// ============================================================================

// Where `needle` first appears at or after `from`, or -1. `from` below 0 is read as 0.
//
// This is what a loop over every occurrence needs, and without it a caller writes
// `string_find(substring(text, from, ...), needle) + from` — which allocates a copy of the rest of
// the text on every iteration and makes the loop quadratic. That is the whole reason it exists.
pure function string_find_from(text: String, needle: String, from: Int) -> Int {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let width: Int = len(needle);
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    if i < 0 {
        i = 0;
    }
    if width == 0 {
        if i > stop {
            return stop;
        }
        return i;
    }
    while i + width <= stop {
        if string_matches_at(text, needle, i) {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Where `needle` LAST appears, or -1. Scanned from the right, so it stops at the first hit rather
// than finding every occurrence and keeping the last.
pure function string_rfind(text: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let width: Int = len(needle);
    if width == 0 {
        return stop;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = stop - width;
    while i >= 0 {
        if string_matches_at(text, needle, i) {
            return i;
        }
        i -= 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// How many NON-OVERLAPPING occurrences of `needle`. `string_count("aaaa", "aa")` is 2, not 3.
//
// Non-overlapping because that is what `string_replace` does, and a `count` that disagreed with the
// `replace` beside it would be a trap: a caller sizing something by `count` and then calling
// `replace` would get a different number of substitutions than it was told.
pure function string_count(text: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    let width: Int = len(needle);
    if width == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable found: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i + width <= stop {
        if string_matches_at(text, needle, i) {
            found += 1;
            i += width;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return found;
}

// Split on RUNS of whitespace, dropping empty pieces. What `split` on a single space cannot do.
//
// `string_split(" a  b ", " ")` answers five pieces, three of them empty. This answers `["a", "b"]`,
// which is what every program reading a table, a log line or a command line actually wants — and
// the difference is not a convenience: a caller using `string_split` on space has to filter the
// empties itself, and the version that forgets is a program that breaks on a double space.
//
// Leading and trailing whitespace therefore produce NO empty piece, and `""` and `"   "` both
// answer an empty list. This is Python's `.split()` with no argument, and Rust's
// `split_whitespace` — the one shape everybody agrees on.
function string_split_space(text: String) -> [String] {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        while i < stop && string_is_space(byte_at(text, i)) {
            i += 1;
        }
        if i < stop {
            let from: Int = i;
            while i < stop && !string_is_space(byte_at(text, i)) {
                i += 1;
            }
            push(pieces, substring(text, from, i - from));
        }
    }
    return pieces;
}

// The text cut at the FIRST separator, as a `(before, after)` pair — or None if the separator is
// not there.
//
// **An `Option`, unlike `string_strip_prefix`, and the difference is that there is no sensible pair
// to answer when the separator is absent.** `("key", "")` would be a lie about a line with no `=`
// in it, and it is exactly the lie that turns a malformed config file into a program with an empty
// password. This is the shape a `key=value` reader wants:
//
//     match string_split_once(line, "=") {
//         None       => { print_error("no = in: " + line); }
//         Some(pair) => { set(pair.0, pair.1); }
//     }
//
// The separator itself is in NEITHER half. An empty separator answers None, agreeing with the rest
// of the file.
function string_split_once(text: String, separator: String) -> Option<(String, String)> {
    let width: Int = len(separator);
    if width == 0 {
        return Option.None;
    }
    let at: Int = string_find(text, separator);
    if at < 0 {
        return Option.None;
    }
    let after: Int = at + width;
    return Option.Some((substring(text, 0, at),
                        substring(text, after, len(text) - after)));
}

// `string_split`, scanned from the RIGHT, pieces answered last-first.
//
// **It is NOT `string_split`'s answer reversed, and that is measured rather than assumed.** For a
// separator that can overlap itself, which occurrences match depends on which end you start from:
//
//     string_split("aaa", "aa")            ->  ["", "a"]   matched at 0; "a" is the TAIL
//     that list, reversed                  ->  ["a", ""]
//     string_rsplit("aaa", "aa")           ->  ["", "a"]   matched at 1; "a" is the HEAD
//
// So the two lists differ — `["a", ""]` against `["", "a"]` — and even where they print alike the
// pieces mean opposite things: the empty one is the tail of a left scan and the head of a right
// one. The fixture prints all three lists side by side rather than leaving that to this comment.
//
// For an ordinary non-overlapping separator this IS `string_split` read backwards, which is the
// common case and the reason to have it: the last field of a dotted name is
// `string_rsplit(name, ".")[0]`, with no need to know how many fields there were.
function string_rsplit(text: String, separator: String) -> [String] {
    let mutable pieces: [String] = [];
    let width: Int = len(separator);
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    if width == 0 {
        push(pieces, text);
        return pieces;
    }
    let mutable to: Int = stop;
    let mutable i: Int = stop - width;
    while i >= 0 {
        if string_matches_at(text, separator, i) {
            let after: Int = i + width;
            push(pieces, substring(text, after, to - after));
            to = i;
            i -= width;
        } else {
            i -= 1;
        }
    }
    push(pieces, substring(text, 0, to));
    return pieces;
}

// `string_split`, with the empty pieces dropped.
//
// The difference matters most at the ends. `string_split("a,,b,", ",")` answers
// `["a", "", "b", ""]` — four fields, which is what a CSV reader must see — and this answers
// `["a", "b"]`, which is what a reader of a `PATH`-style list wants. Two functions because the two
// callers need different things and neither can be derived from the other cheaply by hand: a
// caller who filters `string_split` themselves and forgets is a program that breaks on `"a,,b"`.
function string_split_no_empty(text: String, separator: String) -> [String] {
    let pieces: [String] = string_split(text, separator);
    let count: Int = len(pieces);
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < count {
        if len(pieces[i]) > 0 {
            push(out, pieces[i]);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// ============================================================================
// §D1c — CLASSIFY
//
// **Two families, and the parameter type says which.** A `string_is_*` takes a BYTE, like
// `string_is_space` above it, which is the precedent this file already set. `all_*` and
// `is_ascii` take the whole String. So `string_is_digit(byte_at(s, 0))` asks about one byte and
// `all_digits(s)` asks about every byte, and no call site is ambiguous about which it meant.
//
// **D1c's `all_alpha` is not written**, because `is_alpha` earlier in this file already is it — a
// whole-String "is every byte a letter". That name is out of step with `all_digits` beside it and
// renaming it would be right in isolation; it is left alone because it is a shipped, documented,
// fixture-pinned name and a library-only change is the wrong place to break one. Recorded so that
// "we decided" and "we missed it" do not look the same from outside.
//
// Every `all_*` is true of the empty String — the vacuous answer, and the one that composes. A
// caller who means "a non-empty run of digits" checks the length too, which is clearer at the call
// than hidden in here.
// ============================================================================

// `0`..`9`.
pure function string_is_digit(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return byte >= 48 && byte <= 57;
}

// `A`..`Z` or `a`..`z`. ASCII only: no byte of a multi-byte character is a letter to this.
pure function string_is_alpha(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return (byte >= 65 && byte <= 90) || (byte >= 97 && byte <= 122);
}

// A letter or a digit, ASCII.
pure function string_is_alnum(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return string_is_alpha(byte) || string_is_digit(byte);
}

// `A`..`Z`.
pure function string_is_upper(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return byte >= 65 && byte <= 90;
}

// `a`..`z`.
pure function string_is_lower(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return byte >= 97 && byte <= 122;
}

// `0`..`9`, `a`..`f` or `A`..`F`.
pure function string_is_hex_digit(byte: Int) -> Bool {
    return string_is_digit(byte)
        || (byte >= 97 && byte <= 102)
        || (byte >= 65 && byte <= 70);
}

// The value of a digit byte in base 36: `0`..`9` is 0..9, `a`/`A` is 10, `z`/`Z` is 35, and
// **anything else is -1**.
//
// -1 rather than an `Option`, and this is the one place in the file where that is the right call: it
// is the inner loop of every parse below, an `Option` per byte would allocate nothing but would
// cost a `match` per digit, and the caller's very next line is a range check against the base
// anyway — `digit < 0 || digit >= base` catches "not a digit" and "not a digit IN THIS BASE" in one
// comparison. The sentinel is safe here because -1 is outside every base's digit range, so a caller
// who forgets the check gets a refusal from the range test rather than a wrong number.
pure function string_digit_value(byte: Int) -> Int {
    if byte >= 48 && byte <= 57 {
        return byte - 48;
    }
    if byte >= 97 && byte <= 122 {
        return byte - 87;
    }
    if byte >= 65 && byte <= 90 {
        return byte - 55;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Empty, or nothing but whitespace. The check a program reads a config file with, and the reason
// it is not `string_trim(line) == ""`: that builds a String to answer a Bool, and on a large file
// it does it once per line.
pure function string_is_blank(text: String) -> Bool {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        if !string_is_space(byte_at(text, i)) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Is every byte an ASCII letter or digit? Named for `all_digits`, not for `string_is_alnum`.
pure function all_alnum(text: String) -> Bool {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        if !string_is_alnum(byte_at(text, i)) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Is every byte `0`..`9`, `a`..`f` or `A`..`F`? Named for `all_digits` for the same reason.
pure function all_hex_digits(text: String) -> Bool {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        if !string_is_hex_digit(byte_at(text, i)) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// ============================================================================
// §D1d — PARSE AND FORMAT
//
// ---- the bug this section was written on top of, and it was in THIS file ----
//
// `string_parse_int("99999999999999999999")` **killed the process.** Not a wrong answer — a
// `burxt runtime error: arithmetic overflow` and exit code 70, measured, in an `Option`-returning
// parser whose entire purpose is to answer None for input it cannot read. Burxt's `*` traps, and
// the accumulator was `value = value * 10 + (b - 48)` with nothing in front of it, so any number of
// more than nineteen digits aborted the program.
//
// That is worse than the silent 0 this function was fixed for in v0.0.152, because the caller
// cannot even catch it: a config file, a CSV field or a query parameter with twenty digits in it
// takes the whole program down, and the function's own comment says it exists so that a program
// "has to say what it does about bad input". It could say nothing, because it was never asked.
//
// **The fix is `string_parse_int_base` below, and `string_parse_int` is now one line on top of it.**
// One parser rather than two, so the overflow rule cannot be right in one and missing in the other.
//
// ---- how the overflow guard works, and why the accumulator is NEGATIVE ----
//
// Every step must be checked BEFORE it happens, because a trap cannot be recovered from — this is
// the same ordering `lib/math.bx`'s `checked_*` family is built on and for the same reason.
//
// The accumulator runs DOWN from 0 rather than up, which is not a stylistic choice: `INT_MIN`'s
// magnitude is 2^63 and **that is not an Int**, so a positive accumulator physically cannot hold
// the value while parsing `"-9223372036854775808"`. Counting negative, every intermediate value is
// representable, and the single unrepresentable case — a positive `+9223372036854775808` — is the
// one refusal at the very end.
// ============================================================================

// A number in any base from 2 to 36, or None. Digits are `0`..`9` then `a`..`z`, case-insensitive.
//
// An optional leading `-` or `+`. **Accepting `+` is a change from what `string_parse_int` used to
// do**, and it is deliberate: C, Rust and Python all accept it, and the alternative was one parser
// in this file that took `"+5"` and another that did not.
//
// Refuses, rather than traps or truncates: an empty String, a sign with no digits after it, a byte
// that is not a digit in this base, and anything whose value does not fit an Int. There is no
// whitespace tolerance — `string_trim` is one call and doing it silently would make `" 1 2"` a
// question this function has to have an opinion about.
pure function string_parse_int_base(text: String, base: Int) -> Option<Int>
    requires base >= 2
    requires base <= 36
{
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    if stop == 0 {
        return Option.None;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    let mutable negative: Bool = false;
    let lead: Int = byte_at(text, 0);
    if lead == 45 {
        negative = true;
        i = 1;
    } else if lead == 43 {
        i = 1;
    }
    // A sign alone is not a number, which the pre-v0.0.152 version read as 0.
    if i >= stop {
        return Option.None;
    }
    // `ceil(INT_MIN / base)`: the most negative accumulator that can still be multiplied by `base`
    // without trapping. `divide_floor(a + base - 1, base)` is the ceiling for a positive divisor,
    // and `INT_MIN + base - 1` cannot overflow because `base` is at least 2.
    let smallest: Int = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1;
    let multipliable: Int = divide_floor(smallest + base - 1, base);
    let mutable value: Int = 0;
    while i < stop {
        let digit: Int = string_digit_value(byte_at(text, i));
        if digit < 0 || digit >= base {
            return Option.None;
        }
        // Guard, then multiply. The other order is a trap, not a check.
        if value < multipliable {
            return Option.None;
        }
        let scaled: Int = value * base;
        // Same again for the subtraction. `smallest + digit` is safe because `digit` is >= 0.
        if scaled < smallest + digit {
            return Option.None;
        }
        value = scaled - digit;
        i += 1;
    }
    if negative {
        return Option.Some(value);
    }
    // The one asymmetry in two's complement: `-INT_MIN` has no Int. `"-9223372036854775808"` is a
    // number and `"9223372036854775808"` is not.
    if value == smallest {
        return Option.None;
    }
    return Option.Some(0 - value);
}

// Hexadecimal, with an optional `0x` or `0X` prefix. `string_parse_hex("0xff")` and
// `string_parse_hex("FF")` are both 255.
//
// **The prefix is the only reason this exists** rather than being `string_parse_int_base(s, 16)`
// under a second name — which the project's one-spelling-per-concept rule would refuse. A sign goes
// before the prefix: `"-0x10"` is -16, and `"0x-10"` is not a number.
pure function string_parse_hex(text: String) -> Option<Int> {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable sign: String = "";
    if stop > 0 && byte_at(text, 0) == 45 {
        sign = "-";
        at = 1;
    } else if stop > 0 && byte_at(text, 0) == 43 {
        at = 1;
    }
    if stop >= at + 2 && byte_at(text, at) == 48
        && (byte_at(text, at + 1) == 120 || byte_at(text, at + 1) == 88) {
        let from: Int = at + 2;
        let body: String = substring(text, from, stop - from);
        // **The digits after the prefix must be digits and nothing else.** Handing the body
        // straight to `string_parse_int_base` would accept `"0x-10"` as -16, because that parser
        // reads a sign of its own — measured, and the fixture pins it. A sign belongs before the
        // prefix. The length test is separate because `all_hex_digits("")` is vacuously true, so
        // `"0x"` alone would otherwise pass this guard on its way to being refused later.
        if len(body) == 0 || !all_hex_digits(body) {
            return Option.None;
        }
        return string_parse_int_base(sign + body, 16);
    }
    return string_parse_int_base(text, 16);
}

// An Int written in any base from 2 to 36, lower case, with no prefix. `string_int_to_base(255, 16)`
// is `"ff"`.
//
// ---- INT_MIN, which is where a naive version is wrong ----
//
// Digits come off the NEGATIVE side — `remainder` of a negative is negative here, so `0 - remainder`
// is the digit — and the reason is the same one the parser has: `0 - INT_MIN` traps, so a version
// that took the absolute value first would abort on exactly one input out of 2^64. The fixture
// checks `string_int_to_base(INT_MIN, 10)` for that reason.
//
// ---- the prepend, and why it is not a chunk list ----
//
// `out = digit + out` is the quadratic shape §D0 exists to refuse, and it is correct here for a
// reason that has to be stated or it will be "fixed" into a chunk list by someone reading the rule
// and not the bound: **the digit count is at most 64**, at base 2, for any Int. So the total copied
// is at most 64*65/2 = 2,080 bytes, whatever the value. It is a constant, not a growth rate.
pure function string_int_to_base(value: Int, base: Int) -> String
    requires base >= 2
    requires base <= 36
{
    if value == 0 {
        return "0";
    }
    let digits: String = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
    let mutable n: Int = value;
    if value > 0 {
        n = 0 - value;
    }
    let mutable out: String = "";
    while n != 0 {
        out = substring(digits, 0 - remainder(n, base), 1) + out;
        n = divide_toward_zero(n, base);
    }
    if value < 0 {
        return "-" + out;
    }
    return out;
}

// Base 16, lower case, no `0x`. `string_int_to_hex(255)` is `"ff"`.
//
// A named base rather than a second spelling of `string_int_to_base`: the base is the argument a
// reader cannot check by eye, and `string_int_to_hex(x)` cannot be miswritten as base 61. The same
// argument Rust's `{:x}` makes against `{:radix$}`. Upper case is `string_to_upper_ascii` of this.
pure function string_int_to_hex(value: Int) -> String {
    return string_int_to_base(value, 16);
}

// Base 2, no `0b`. `string_int_to_binary(5)` is `"101"`.
pure function string_int_to_binary(value: Int) -> String {
    return string_int_to_base(value, 2);
}

// An Int zero-padded to at least `width` characters. `string_int_padded(7, 3)` is `"007"`.
//
// **The sign goes before the zeros and counts toward the width**, so `string_int_padded(-7, 4)` is
// `"-007"` and not `"0-07"` or `"-0007"`. That is printf's `%04d` and every other language's
// answer; the alternatives are respectively unreadable and off by one.
//
// A number already that wide is unchanged — never truncated. Padding that silently dropped a digit
// would be a wrong number in a column that looks right, which is the failure this language exists
// to refuse.
pure function string_int_padded(value: Int, width: Int) -> String {
    let body: String = to_string(value);
    let have: Int = len(body);
    if have >= width {
        return body;
    }
    if value < 0 {
        return "-" + string_repeat("0", width - have) + substring(body, 1, have - 1);
    }
    return string_repeat("0", width - have) + body;
}
